---

<ArticleLayout
  meta={meta}
  faqs={[
    {
      q: 'What kind of power cord does the PS5 use?',
      a: 'The PS5 uses a standard IEC C7 "figure-8" two-prong power cord — the same non-polarized connector used by the PS4, PS3 Slim, Xbox Series S/X, many TVs, printers, and speakers. It is not a proprietary Sony cable.',
    },
    {
      q: 'How much does a PS5 power cord cost?',
      a: 'A third-party replacement costs about $5 to $10 on Amazon for a single cord, or around $8 to $9 for a two-pack. You do not need to pay a premium for a Sony-branded cord because the connector is a universal standard.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Can I use my PS4 power cord on the PS5?',
      a: 'Yes. The PS4, PS4 Slim, PS4 Pro, and PS5 all use the same C7 figure-8 connector, so a PS4 power cord works perfectly on a PS5. This is the fastest fix if you have an old cable lying around.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Do I need a specific length?',
      a: 'It depends on your setup. The original PS5 cord is about 5 feet. If your console sits far from the outlet, a 6.6-foot or 10-foot cord is more convenient. A 3-foot cord is fine if the console is right next to the socket.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Is a cheap power cord safe?',
      a: 'As long as it is UL or ETL listed and uses 18 AWG wire with a 7A/125V rating, a cheap cord is completely safe. Avoid uncertified no-name cables, but a $5–$10 listed cord is perfectly fine for the PS5.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Where is the PS5 power port located?',
      a: 'The AC power inlet is on the lower rear of the console, next to the HDMI and Ethernet ports. The C7 connector slides straight in — you cannot plug it in the wrong way because it is non-polarized.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Does the PS5 work on 100–240V worldwide?',
      a: 'Yes. The PS5 has a universal power supply rated for 100–240V, so the same C7 cord works in North America, Europe, and Asia. You only need the correct wall plug for your region, which is built into the cord.',
    },
  ]}
>
  <p>
    If you have lost or damaged your PS5 power cord, the good news is that replacing it
    is cheap and easy — far easier than most people expect. The PS5 uses a standard
    <strong>IEC C7 "figure-8" two-prong cable</strong>, the same connector found on the PS4,
    Xbox Series S/X, countless TVs, and half the small appliances in your house. There is
    nothing proprietary about it, which means a $5–$10 third-party replacement works
    exactly as well as the original. Here is everything you need to know to buy the right
    cord the first time.
  </p>

  <h2>The short version: you do not need a Sony cord</h2>

  <p>
    Sony does not even sell a standalone PS5 power cord as a major accessory, and you
    would not want to pay a premium for one if they did. Because the PS5 uses a universal
    C7 connector, any certified replacement with the right specs will do the job
    perfectly. The most important thing to understand is that a power cord is a power
    cord — the console's real power supply is inside the machine, so the external cable is
    just a simple conductor with a standardized plug at each end.
  </p>

  <figure class="article-figure">
    <img
      src="/images/ps5-power-cord-c7.svg"
      alt="The IEC C7 figure-8 connector and the specs to look for in a PS5 power cord"
      loading="lazy"
      width="800"
      height="450"
    />
    <figcaption>The PS5 uses a standard IEC C7 figure-8 connector. Any 18 AWG, 7A/125V, UL or ETL listed cord works.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>What to look for in a replacement</h2>

  <p>
    Not every cord on the shelf is created equal, but the bar is low and easy to meet.
    Look for three things. First, the connector should be a <strong>C7 (figure-8)
    two-prong</strong> — not the larger three-prong "mickey mouse" C5 that some laptops use.
    Second, the wire should be <strong>18 AWG</strong> with a <strong>7A/125V</strong> rating,
    which is more than enough for the PS5's roughly 200-watt draw. Third, and most
    important, the cord should be <strong>UL or ETL listed</strong>, which means it has passed
    basic safety testing. Beyond that, it is just a question of length.
  </p>

  <h2>What a PS5 power cord actually costs</h2>

  <p>
    This is where it gets reassuring. Real third-party C7 cords on Amazon currently run
    about <strong>$5 to $10</strong> for a single cable, with two-packs priced around
    <strong>$8 to $9</strong> — roughly $4 per cord. Longer cables and brand-name options sit
    at the top of that range, while shorter no-frills cords sit at the bottom. In other
    words, a full replacement costs less than a sandwich, and a spare for the drawer costs
    almost nothing.
  </p>

  <figure class="article-figure">
    <img
      src="/images/ps5-power-cord-buy.svg"
      alt="PS5 power cord prices: single cords $5-10, two-packs $8-9, and how to pick the right length"
      loading="lazy"
      width="800"
      height="450"
    />
    <figcaption>A single replacement runs $5–$10, a two-pack about $8–$9, and lengths go from 3 to 10 feet.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>Picking the right length</h2>

  <p>
    The original PS5 ships with a cord around 5 feet long, which is fine for a console
    near the outlet but falls short in many living-room setups. If your TV unit is against
    a wall with the outlet behind it, a <strong>3-foot cord</strong> keeps things tidy. For a
    typical entertainment center, a <strong>6.6-foot cord</strong> is the sweet spot and the
    most popular length. If the nearest outlet is on the far side of the room, grab a
    <strong>10-foot cord</strong> rather than daisy-chaining extension cables. Measure the
    distance from your console to the socket and round up.
  </p>

  <h2>Compatibility: it works with almost everything</h2>

  <p>
    Because the C7 figure-8 connector is a universal standard, a PS5 power cord is
    genuinely interchangeable with a huge amount of gear. It works with the PS4, PS4 Slim,
    PS4 Pro, and PS3 Slim, with the Xbox Series S and X, and with the Xbox One S and One X.
    It also powers many TVs, monitors, printers, speakers, and audio equipment. That cuts
    both ways: if you have an old PS4 cable in a drawer, it will power your PS5 right now
    with zero cost.
  </p>

  <h2>Voltage: one cord works worldwide</h2>

  <p>
    One more thing worth knowing before you buy. The PS5 has a universal power supply
    rated for <strong>100–240V</strong>, so the console itself works in North America, Europe,
    Asia, and everywhere in between. The only regional difference is the wall plug — a US
    cord has two flat prongs, a UK cord has three, and a European cord has two round pins.
    As long as you buy a cord with the wall plug for your     country, the console side is
    identical.
  </p>

  <h2>Is the power cord actually the problem?</h2>

  <p>
    Before you order a new cord, it is worth confirming the cord is really the culprit.
    If your PS5 shows no power at all, the issue could be the cord, the wall outlet, or
    the console&apos;s internal power supply. The fastest way to test is to try a different
    C7 cord — any PS4 or TV power cable works — and a different outlet. If the console
    still will not turn on with a known-good cord and outlet, the problem is inside the
    machine, and a new power cord will not fix it.
  </p>

  <p>
    The most common cord failures are easy to spot: a frayed or split jacket near the
    plug, a bent or damaged prong, or a cord that has been crushed under furniture. If
    you see exposed wire, stop using it immediately. These physical problems are the
    typical reason a power cord needs replacing, and they are far more common than a
    hidden internal fault.
  </p>

  <h2>How to buy the right cord</h2>

  <p>
    To summarize the whole decision into a checklist: confirm the connector is a
    <strong>two-prong C7 figure-8</strong>, check the wire is <strong>18 AWG</strong> and rated
    <strong>7A/125V</strong>, make sure it is <strong>UL or ETL listed</strong>, and pick a
    <strong>length</strong> that comfortably reaches your outlet. Match those four things and
    any $5–$10 cord will do the job. There is no performance difference between a cheap
    listed cord and an expensive one — you are paying for length, brand, and packaging,
    not better power delivery.
  </p>

  <h2>Well-reviewed replacements to consider</h2>

  <p>
    If you want a specific recommendation rather than shopping blind, a few names stand
    out. <strong>TPLTECH</strong> C7 cords are among the most popular on Amazon, with UL
    certification and lengths from 3 to 6.5 feet, and they are consistently well-reviewed.
    <strong>Superer</strong> and <strong>Goalfish</strong> both sell ETL-listed figure-8 cables in
    6.6-foot and 10-foot lengths that are equally solid. For a spare you can forget about,
    a <strong>two-pack</strong> from any of these brands costs about $8 to $9 and covers both
    your console and a backup. Any of them will power a PS5 without issue — the differences
    come down to length, certification badge, and whether you want a second cable in the box.
  </p>

  <h2>Bottom line</h2>

  <p>
    A PS5 power cord is one of the easiest and cheapest things to replace on the console.
    It is a standard C7 figure-8 cable, so you can use an old PS4 cord, a spare from your
    TV, or a $5–$10 certified replacement from Amazon — all of them work identically. Match
    the connector, wire gauge, and safety listing, pick a comfortable length, and you are
    back up and running for less than the cost of a fast-food meal. Check the current
    prices below to grab a replacement today.
  </p>
